{"module":"policing","voice":"female","duration":216.5,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Policing and the 911 call","start":0.0,"end":2.58,"words":[[0.0,0.5],[0.5,0.72],[0.72,0.86],[0.86,2.23],[2.23,2.44]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The CAREN Act is about one specific harm: calling the police on someone because of their race.","start":3.38,"end":8.57,"words":[[3.43,3.56],[3.56,3.9],[3.9,4.1],[4.1,4.23],[4.23,4.56],[4.56,4.76],[4.76,5.3],[5.3,5.57],[5.8,6.14],[6.14,6.27],[6.27,6.6],[6.6,6.74],[6.74,7.14],[7.14,7.47],[7.47,7.61],[7.61,7.81],[7.81,8.14]]},{"el":"intro","text":"This module shows where that harm comes from and why a phone call can be dangerous.","start":8.92,"end":13.98,"words":[[8.97,9.19],[9.19,9.63],[9.63,9.93],[9.93,10.15],[10.15,10.37],[10.37,10.59],[10.59,10.89],[10.89,11.18],[11.18,11.4],[11.4,11.62],[11.62,11.77],[11.77,12.07],[12.07,12.29],[12.29,12.51],[12.51,12.66],[12.66,13.47]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Slave patrols","start":14.33,"end":15.95,"words":[[14.35,14.91],[14.91,15.82]]},{"el":"s0","text":"From South Carolina's 1704 law onward, Southern colonies and states organized slave patrols: white men who rode at night, stopped Black people, demanded passes, searched slave quarters and whipped people found without permission.","start":16.65,"end":33.13,"words":[[16.69,17.02],[17.02,17.35],[17.35,18.17],[18.17,19.15],[19.15,19.31],[19.31,19.8],[20.09,20.5],[20.5,21.07],[21.07,21.31],[21.31,21.8],[21.8,22.46],[22.46,22.87],[22.87,23.6],[23.89,24.22],[24.22,24.46],[24.46,24.63],[24.63,24.95],[24.95,25.12],[25.12,25.53],[25.81,26.22],[26.22,26.55],[26.55,27.04],[27.32,27.98],[27.98,28.47],[28.76,29.16],[29.16,29.57],[29.57,30.06],[30.06,30.31],[30.31,30.64],[30.64,31.04],[31.04,31.45],[31.45,31.94],[31.94,32.6]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Every white person was expected to help police enslaved people.","start":33.48,"end":37.16,"words":[[33.53,33.79],[33.79,34.04],[34.04,34.36],[34.36,34.55],[34.55,35.13],[35.13,35.26],[35.26,35.51],[35.51,35.83],[35.83,36.35],[36.35,36.73]]},{"el":"h1","text":"From patrols to police","start":38.06,"end":39.97,"words":[[38.06,38.43],[38.43,39.16],[39.16,39.35],[39.35,39.81]]},{"el":"s1","text":"After emancipation, many Southern patrol duties passed to new city police and sheriffs.","start":40.67,"end":46.87,"words":[[40.72,41.04],[41.04,42.08],[42.36,42.67],[42.67,43.07],[43.07,43.63],[43.63,44.11],[44.11,44.42],[44.42,44.58],[44.58,44.82],[44.82,45.14],[45.14,45.54],[45.54,45.78],[45.78,46.33]]},{"el":"s1","text":"How direct the line is from patrols to modern policing is debated: Northern departments (Boston 1838, New York 1845) grew mostly from London's model and from controlling immigrants and labor.","start":47.22,"end":62.89,"words":[[47.22,47.48],[47.48,48.06],[48.06,48.23],[48.23,48.56],[48.56,48.73],[48.73,49.07],[49.07,49.74],[49.74,49.9],[49.9,50.32],[50.32,50.91],[50.91,51.08],[51.08,51.83],[52.12,52.54],[52.54,53.38],[53.38,53.88],[53.88,55.05],[55.35,55.6],[55.6,55.85],[55.85,56.94],[57.23,57.48],[57.48,58.07],[58.07,58.4],[58.4,58.99],[58.99,59.41],[59.41,59.66],[59.66,60.0],[60.0,60.83],[60.83,61.59],[61.59,61.84],[61.84,62.34]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Historians agree that in the South, the job of controlling Black movement carried over.","start":63.24,"end":68.55,"words":[[63.29,63.98],[63.98,64.26],[64.26,64.47],[64.47,64.61],[64.61,64.75],[64.75,65.1],[65.34,65.48],[65.48,65.76],[65.76,65.9],[65.9,66.59],[66.59,66.87],[66.87,67.36],[67.36,67.71],[67.71,68.06]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Black Codes, vagrancy laws and convict leasing","start":69.45,"end":72.99,"words":[[69.5,69.84],[69.84,70.34],[70.63,71.38],[71.38,71.63],[71.63,71.88],[71.88,72.46],[72.46,72.88]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The 13th Amendment (1865) abolished slavery “except as a punishment for crime.”","start":73.69,"end":80.44,"words":[[73.74,73.9],[73.9,74.38],[74.38,75.1],[75.1,76.3],[76.58,77.14],[77.14,77.78],[77.78,78.26],[78.26,78.42],[78.42,78.58],[78.58,79.3],[79.3,79.46],[79.46,79.94]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Southern states immediately passed Black Codes making it a crime for Black people to be unemployed, to walk at night, or to “loiter.”","start":80.79,"end":88.75,"words":[[80.83,81.19],[81.19,81.63],[81.63,82.35],[82.35,82.64],[82.64,82.93],[82.93,83.29],[83.29,83.72],[83.72,83.87],[83.87,84.01],[84.01,84.38],[84.38,84.52],[84.52,84.81],[84.81,85.17],[85.17,85.32],[85.32,85.46],[85.46,86.18],[86.44,86.58],[86.58,86.8],[86.8,86.94],[86.94,87.31],[87.56,87.63],[87.63,87.78],[87.78,88.21]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Those arrested were leased to plantations, mines and railroads, often dying at higher rates than under slavery.","start":89.1,"end":96.14,"words":[[89.15,89.44],[89.44,89.94],[89.94,90.08],[90.08,90.36],[90.36,90.51],[90.51,91.36],[91.61,91.96],[91.96,92.18],[92.18,92.89],[93.13,93.42],[93.42,93.77],[93.77,93.92],[93.92,94.2],[94.2,94.56],[94.56,94.77],[94.77,95.05],[95.05,95.69]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Convict leasing lasted in Alabama until 1928.","start":96.49,"end":100.68,"words":[[96.49,97.01],[97.01,97.37],[97.37,97.82],[97.82,97.96],[97.96,98.48],[98.48,98.85],[98.85,100.17]]},{"el":"h3","text":"An accusation could be a death sentence","start":101.58,"end":104.25,"words":[[101.63,101.8],[101.8,102.66],[102.66,102.92],[102.92,103.09],[103.09,103.27],[103.27,103.52],[103.52,104.13]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Many lynchings began with an accusation from a white person.","start":104.95,"end":108.75,"words":[[105.0,105.28],[105.28,105.82],[105.82,106.16],[106.16,106.37],[106.37,106.51],[106.51,107.19],[107.19,107.46],[107.46,107.6],[107.6,107.87],[107.87,108.28]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 1955 Carolyn Bryant's account of an encounter in a Mississippi store led to the murder of 14-year-old Emmett Till.","start":109.1,"end":117.32,"words":[[109.15,109.3],[109.3,110.54],[110.54,111.08],[111.08,111.7],[111.7,112.16],[112.16,112.32],[112.32,112.47],[112.47,113.09],[113.09,113.24],[113.24,113.4],[113.4,114.02],[114.02,114.25],[114.25,114.48],[114.48,114.63],[114.63,114.79],[114.79,115.1],[115.1,115.25],[115.25,116.26],[116.26,116.56],[116.56,116.87]]},{"el":"s3","text":"(In a 2017 book, historian Timothy Tyson reported that she later admitted parts of her account were untrue; her family disputes this.","start":117.67,"end":126.99,"words":[[117.72,117.87],[117.87,118.02],[118.02,119.08],[119.08,119.38],[119.64,120.32],[120.32,120.78],[120.78,121.23],[121.23,121.76],[121.76,121.99],[121.99,122.14],[122.14,122.51],[122.51,123.04],[123.04,123.35],[123.35,123.5],[123.5,123.65],[123.65,124.1],[124.1,124.25],[124.25,124.7],[124.97,125.12],[125.12,125.57],[125.57,126.18],[126.18,126.48]]},{"el":"s3","text":"That detail is debated.)","start":127.34,"end":129.22,"words":[[127.39,127.61],[127.61,128.04],[128.04,128.19],[128.19,128.84]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The lesson the CAREN Act builds on is older than any single case: when the person called about is Black, the police response can be deadly.","start":129.57,"end":137.52,"words":[[129.62,129.76],[129.76,130.11],[130.11,130.25],[130.25,130.6],[130.6,130.81],[130.81,131.16],[131.16,131.3],[131.3,131.44],[131.44,131.79],[131.79,132.0],[132.0,132.21],[132.21,132.63],[132.63,132.98],[133.23,133.44],[133.44,133.58],[133.58,133.93],[133.93,134.21],[134.21,134.56],[134.56,134.7],[134.7,135.05],[135.29,135.43],[135.43,135.78],[135.78,136.27],[136.27,136.48],[136.48,136.62],[136.62,137.04]]},{"el":"h4","text":"The weaponized 911 call today","start":138.42,"end":140.8,"words":[[138.46,138.58],[138.58,139.11],[139.11,140.23],[140.23,140.41],[140.41,140.7]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 2018 and 2020, videos showed white callers phoning police about Black people barbecuing (Oakland), waiting in a Starbucks (Philadelphia, where two men were arrested), selling water (San Francisco) and birdwatching (Central Park).","start":141.5,"end":156.42,"words":[[141.55,141.7],[141.7,142.55],[142.55,142.76],[142.76,143.69],[143.94,144.5],[144.5,144.79],[144.79,145.07],[145.07,145.43],[145.43,145.85],[145.85,146.21],[146.21,146.56],[146.56,146.85],[146.85,147.2],[147.2,147.84],[147.84,148.41],[148.66,149.09],[149.09,149.23],[149.23,149.37],[149.37,149.87],[149.87,150.65],[150.9,151.11],[151.11,151.26],[151.26,151.47],[151.47,151.61],[151.61,152.18],[152.43,152.78],[152.78,153.07],[153.07,153.28],[153.28,153.99],[154.24,154.45],[154.45,155.16],[155.16,155.66],[155.66,155.95]]},{"el":"s4","text":"San Francisco passed the CAREN Act in 2020, following Oregon's 2019 law and California's AB 1775.","start":156.77,"end":165.72,"words":[[156.77,157.0],[157.0,157.77],[157.77,158.08],[158.08,158.23],[158.23,158.62],[158.62,158.85],[158.85,159.0],[159.0,160.0],[160.27,160.81],[160.81,161.35],[161.35,162.35],[162.35,162.5],[162.5,162.73],[162.73,163.5],[163.5,163.65],[163.65,165.19]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The data backs up the videos.","start":166.07,"end":167.96,"words":[[166.12,166.24],[166.24,166.53],[166.53,166.76],[166.76,166.87],[166.87,166.99],[166.99,167.5]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In San Francisco, only about 1% of “suspicious person” calls in the past year led to an arrest, citation or report.","start":168.31,"end":177.33,"words":[[168.37,168.53],[168.53,168.78],[168.78,169.69],[169.98,170.4],[170.4,170.81],[170.81,171.56],[171.56,171.72],[171.72,172.39],[172.39,172.8],[172.8,173.13],[173.13,173.3],[173.3,173.47],[173.47,173.8],[173.8,174.05],[174.05,174.29],[174.29,174.46],[174.46,174.63],[174.63,175.12],[175.41,176.16],[176.16,176.24],[176.24,176.74]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The federal Police-Public Contact Survey (2020) found that Black people who had contact with police were nearly three times as likely as white people to experience threats or use of force.","start":177.68,"end":189.07,"words":[[177.72,177.86],[177.86,178.38],[178.38,179.19],[179.19,179.7],[179.7,180.07],[180.07,180.95],[181.21,181.57],[181.57,181.79],[181.79,182.09],[182.09,182.46],[182.46,182.6],[182.6,182.82],[182.82,183.34],[183.34,183.56],[183.56,183.93],[183.93,184.07],[184.07,184.44],[184.44,184.66],[184.66,185.03],[185.03,185.17],[185.17,185.61],[185.61,185.76],[185.76,186.06],[186.06,186.42],[186.42,186.57],[186.57,187.45],[187.45,187.82],[187.82,187.89],[187.89,188.11],[188.11,188.26],[188.26,188.55]]},{"el":"h5","text":"What works","start":189.97,"end":190.89,"words":[[190.02,190.34],[190.34,190.76]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Cities are sending trained responders instead of armed police to many calls.","start":191.59,"end":196.11,"words":[[191.64,192.01],[192.01,192.08],[192.08,192.52],[192.52,192.95],[192.95,193.6],[193.6,194.04],[194.04,194.18],[194.18,194.4],[194.4,194.76],[194.76,194.91],[194.91,195.2],[195.2,195.56]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Eugene, Oregon's CAHOOTS program has done so since 1989.","start":196.46,"end":201.76,"words":[[196.51,197.06],[197.33,197.88],[197.88,198.35],[198.35,198.97],[198.97,199.21],[199.21,199.44],[199.44,199.68],[199.68,199.99],[199.99,201.24]]},{"el":"s5","text":"A 2022 study in Science Advances found that Denver's STAR program reduced reports of the low-level crimes it targeted.","start":202.11,"end":210.5,"words":[[202.16,202.31],[202.31,203.37],[203.37,203.74],[203.74,203.9],[203.9,204.35],[204.35,204.95],[204.95,205.33],[205.33,205.56],[205.56,206.01],[206.01,206.24],[206.24,206.85],[206.85,207.38],[207.38,207.83],[207.83,207.98],[207.98,208.13],[208.13,208.74],[208.74,209.19],[209.19,209.34],[209.34,209.95]]},{"el":"s5","text":"A federal CAREN Act adds the other half: a consequence for using the police as a weapon.","start":210.85,"end":215.57,"words":[[210.9,211.02],[211.02,211.46],[211.46,211.77],[211.77,211.96],[211.96,212.14],[212.14,212.27],[212.27,212.45],[212.45,212.7],[212.92,213.04],[213.04,213.67],[213.67,213.79],[213.79,214.1],[214.1,214.23],[214.23,214.54],[214.54,214.66],[214.66,214.78],[214.78,215.16]]}]}